// pfodWeb NOTE: GPIO0 tagged "button" - inferred BOOT button, moderate-high
// confidence. Vendor documentation (nologo.tech's ESP32S3 Pico getting-
// started guide) confirms this board has a physical BOOT button used to
// enter download mode ("hold BOOT, then press RESET"), but the page text
// does not spell out the GPIO number. On every ESP32-S3 board the ROM
// bootloader's download-mode strap is hard-wired to this specific GPIO
// number by the chip silicon itself (not a board design choice), so any
// board with a working BOOT button must wire it here - consistent with
// this chip's own pinNotes "0": "Must be HIGH for normal boot". No
// independent third-party product page was found to corroborate beyond
// the vendor's own docs, so treat this as inferred-from-architecture
// rather than a directly labelled schematic reference.
// pfodWeb NOTE: GPIO21 tagged "led_neopixel" - high confidence, vendor-
// confirmed. This board's own pin header file wires its built-in RGB LED
// macro to this same GPIO (visible from the LED_BUILTIN/RGB_BUILTIN/
// RGB_BRIGHTNESS trio near the top of the file, the standard Arduino-ESP32
// core pattern for an addressable single-wire RGB LED). Independently,
// nologo.tech's own ESP32S3 Pico documentation states in both its Arduino
// and MicroPython getting-started guides that "the RGB and blue LED share
// GPIO pin 21" and gives a WS2812 example using that same pin with 8
// LEDs, GRB order - matching this board's onboard indicator exactly.
// pfodWeb NOTE: checked for SPI/I2C alias collisions on GPIO0 and GPIO21
// (the "critical check" from the audit brief) - none exist. This file's
// SPI signal aliases are declared as a signed 8-bit type holding a
// negative placeholder value, and its I2C signal aliases are declared as
// the unsigned 8-bit pin type but likewise assigned a negative literal;
// build_boards.js's alias-parsing regex only accepts a bare non-negative
// integer or another identifier on the right-hand side of a `static const
// uint8_t` line, so a negative literal never matches regardless of the
// declared type, and none of these six aliases resolve to a real pin
// number. Verified directly by running the parser's regex against this
// file: only the RGB LED alias and the ten analog aliases produced a
// match. No boilerplate deletion was needed.
